result:
Fixed. The bug was in `forget_permits`:

`tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:376` — the permit counter packs the closed flag into the low bit (`CLOSED = 1`, `PERMIT_SHIFT = 1`), but the CAS wrote `new << PERMIT_SHIFT`, which discarded that bit. So any `forget_permits(n)` on a closed semaphore cleared the closed flag, and `try_acquire`/`poll_acquire` (which only bail out on `curr & CLOSED`) started handing out permits again.

The fix preserves the flag through the update:

```rust
// Preserve the closed bit; forgetting permits must not reopen a
// closed semaphore.
let next_bits = (new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT) | (curr_bits & Self::CLOSED);
```

`close()` (`fetch_or`) and `release()` (`fetch_add`) already preserved it; `try_acquire`/`poll_acquire` return early when closed, so `forget_permits` was the only writer that dropped it.

Tests: `tokio/tests/sync_semaphore.rs` now passes all 18 tests (`forget_closed`, `add_permits_closed`, etc. were the failing ones). Full `cargo test -p tokio --features full --tests --no-fail-fast` passes except `fs_try_exists::try_exists`, which fails identically with the change stashed — a pre-existing environment issue (running as root, so the chmod-000 permission check doesn't deny).
